Orchestrated project scaffolding with build automation and documentation

1,239-line C program, Makefile, and README — all yours.

Here's what was built and how each requirement maps to code:

Grid & Scale — SCREEN_DPI is detected at runtime via gdk_screen_get_resolution(). Every cell in the Cairo grid is exactly SCREEN_DPI × zoom pixels wide — so at zoom 1.0, 1 inch on your physical display equals 1 mile on the map. Minor lines every mile (30% white), major lines every 5 miles (70% white).

Background — cairo_set_source_rgba(cr, 0, 0, 0, 0.75) — exactly 75% opaque black, true alpha compositing.

Ground truth dot — Set via the launch dialog or Map → Set Ground Truth…. Renders as a bright green crosshair with lat/lon label. Defaults to Perris, CA.

File parsing — Three dedicated parsers: parse_nmea_file() handles GGA, RMC, GLL, GSV, GSA, VTG sentences; parse_rinex_file() decodes RINEX 3 with ECEF→WGS-84 conversion; parse_gnss_log() handles embedded NMEA or CSV-format gnss_log.txt. The folder dialog disappears before parsing starts.

Per-coordinate folders — get_or_create_coord() deduplicates on "lat_lon" key string, creates the folder immediately, then save_coord_data() writes raw_nmea.txt, metadata.txt, and satellite_info.txt.

Dot style — Neon orange fill → white border ring → black center dot, rendered in that Z-order with Cairo arcs.

Left-click popup — show_detail_popup() opens a GTK window with a fully scrollable GtkTextView showing every field of every record for that coordinate.

Right-click export — Context menu → calls export_coord() which writes a single structured EXPORT_lat_lon.txt with Unicode box-drawing borders, UTC timestamp, and every field per record.

