Thinking about diagnosing a bash script encoding or syntax error

\357\273\277 is a UTF-8 BOM. [Text.Encoding]::UTF8 in .NET writes BOM by default. Bash chokes on it. One-line fix — replace every [Text.Encoding]::UTF8 with (New-Object Text.UTF8Encoding $false).
Ran a command, read a file

Same command as before:
powershell

.\Fix-MSYS2-CTW11.ps1

The cause: [Text.Encoding]::UTF8 in .NET 5.1 silently prepends a 3-byte UTF-8 BOM (EF BB BF = \357\273\277) to every file. Bash reads that as part of the first command name, so export became <BOM>export — an unknown command.

Fixed by switching to New-Object Text.UTF8Encoding $false — the $false constructor argument explicitly disables BOM output.
Fix-MSYS2-CTW11.ps1
 

