Reconsidered sampling depth for lengthy file headers

Good catch. That header block alone is 50+ lines before a single data row appears. Bumping the sniffer to 200 lines and making it smarter:
Ran 2 commands, read a file

Sniffer now scans up to 500 lines using a score system instead of a single match. Each indicator adds points:
Signal found	Points
"GNSS Logger" anywhere	+5, stop immediately
Version: vX.X Platform: ...	+3
HardwareClockDiscontinuityCount	+3
KlobucharAlpha / SatelliteInterSignalBias	+3 each
Fix,Provider + LatitudeDegrees on same line	+3
Raw,utcTimeMillis + TimeNanos	+2
PseudorangeRateMetersPerSecond	+2
UncalAccel/Gyro/Mag sensor headers	+1
Actual Fix, data row	+2
Complementary combos (fix header + sensors, etc.)	+2 bonus

Threshold is 2 — so even a partial header with just one strong field still triggers the right parser. Also exits early once score hits 4 so it doesn't waste time on files it's already confident about. Detection failures now print the score and lines scanned to stderr so you can see exactly why a file was accepted or rejected.
